            {
              "id": "glog-623f0770-c34a-467e-8f8a-9b379c8292a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS * NB_THREADS_BLOCKS`. If any of these variables contain a large value, the multiplication could result in an integer overflow. This would cause `malloc` to allocate less memory than expected, leading to buffer overflow when the memory is accessed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate the size of the memory to be allocated. Ensure that it is within a reasonable range and that the multiplication does not result in an integer overflow.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Handle the case where `malloc` fails to allocate memory. It returns a null pointer in such cases, and any attempt to access this memory would result in a segmentation fault.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS * NB_THREADS_BLOCKS;\nif (size / OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B / NB_THREADS != NB_THREADS_BLOCKS) {\n    // handle integer overflow\n} else {\n    void* memory = malloc(size);\n    if (memory == NULL) {\n        // handle failed memory allocation\n    } else {\n        // use memory\n        free(memory);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-14253361-a386-4d3f-a37a-7c8a319dd45c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. The `malloc` function is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to potential buffer overflow, integer overflow, or memory corruption issues.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated as `2 * OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS`. If the values of `OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B` or `NB_THREADS` are not properly controlled or validated, it could lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated to prevent integer overflow.\n3. Use secure coding practices to prevent buffer overflow and memory corruption issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA potential fix for the code could be to add checks to ensure that the size of the memory to be allocated does not exceed a certain limit. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n#define MAX_SIZE (INT_MAX / 2)\n\n// ...\n\nif (OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B > MAX_SIZE || NB_THREADS > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: size too large\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(2 * OUTPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS);\n    if (!ptr) {\n        // Handle error: malloc failed\n    }\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-fb4b6447-39ec-420d-af88-7c93c3f9b837",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several security issues such as Denial of Service (DoS), buffer overflow, or memory corruption. In this case, the size of the memory to be allocated is determined by `INPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS*NB_THREADS_BLOCKS * NB_INPUT_BLOCK`, which could potentially be manipulated to cause a vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Validate and sanitize all inputs: Ensure that all input data is validated and sanitized to prevent malicious data from being processed.\n\n2. Use secure functions: Instead of `malloc`, consider using functions like `calloc` which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n3. Limit memory allocation size: Implement checks to ensure that the size of the memory to be allocated does not exceed a certain limit.\n\n4. Error handling: Always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n#define MAX_SIZE 1000000\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t size = INPUT_BLOCK_SIZE_B * NB_THREADS*NB_THREADS_BLOCKS * NB_INPUT_BLOCK;\n\nif (size > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            }
